const body = `Without the \`build.sh\` acquisition block (removed in #24), a clone made without git-lfs is **silent**: the checkout succeeds, \`git status\` is clean, and the fixture files are 131-byte LFS pointers. The failure only surfaces deep inside a test as \`Invalid byte order in TIFF header\`, which points nowhere near the actual cause.

Part of #22 (epic: Revive TiffCraft).

## Proposal

Detect the LFS pointer signature where the fixture is opened, and fail with the remedy.

A small shared helper, called from the fixture-path helpers in both test binaries, so it is cross-platform and needs no script and no CMake change:

\`\`\`cpp
// tests/fixtureCheck.hpp
inline void requireRealFixture(const std::filesystem::path& path) {
  std::ifstream file(path, std::ios::binary);
  char head[64] = {};
  file.read(head, sizeof(head));
  if (std::string(head, file.gcount()).rfind("version https://git-lfs", 0) == 0) {
    throw std::runtime_error(
      "Git LFS pointer file, not a TIFF: " + path.string() +
      "\\nInstall git-lfs and run: git -C tests/libtiff-pics lfs pull");
  }
}
\`\`\`

The point is the message: the developer should read *"install git-lfs and run this command"*, not *"Invalid byte order in TIFF header"*.

## Notes

- The helper only *reads*; it changes nothing in the repository and needs no privileges. It fits the same principle that removed the acquisition block from \`build.sh\`: nothing mutates the user's checkout, and the diagnostic lives in the phase that needs the fixtures.
- CMake stays out of it — 3321333 stands: CMake is for build configuration, not environment validation. This check runs at test time, not configure time.
- CI is unaffected either way; verifying the fixtures are real TIFFs there is epic item C2, which is a separate concern (it asserts on CI's own ephemeral checkout).
- An implementation already exists on branch \`bugfix/issue-24\` (\`tests/fixtureCheck.hpp\` plus the two call sites in \`tests/tiffImageTest.cpp\` and \`tests/tiffExporterTest.cpp\`) and can be used as a starting point.

## Validation

- \`ctest --preset gcc-linux-release --output-on-failure\` → 2/2 pass with real fixtures.
- The diagnostic fires with the actionable message when a fixture is a pointer file. To reproduce: restore one pointer into the fixture submodule (\`git -C tests/libtiff-pics checkout HEAD -- <file>.tif\` after removing the LFS objects, or write the pointer text into a copy of the file) and confirm the failure message names git-lfs instead of the byte order.
- The helper does not change the behaviour for a missing file: an unreadable fixture still fails with the existing "cannot open" error.
`;

